## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an EU-resident analytics flow with data governance, simple serverless components, and cost awareness.

## Key Concepts
- EU residency and data sovereignty
- Event-driven ingestion with Azure Event Hubs
- Serverless processing with Azure Functions
- Per-tenant access control via Cosmos DB RLS
- BYOK using Azure Key Vault
- Data retention and purge workflows
- DR planning and cost optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
module.exports = async function (context, eventHubMessages) {
  const { CosmosClient } = require('@azure/cosmos');
  const client = new CosmosClient(process.env.COSMOS_CONNECTION);
  const container = client.database('Analytics').container('TenantStats');
  for (const msg of eventHubMessages) {
    const payload = JSON.parse(msg.body);
    const item = {
      id: payload.id,
      tenantId: payload.tenantId,
      metric: payload.metric,
      value: payload.value,
      ts: new Date().toISOString()
    };
    await container.items.upsert(item);
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle GDPR data deletion requests in this flow?
- What tests would you run to verify retention and DR requirements?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses your ability to architect a scalable, cross-region analytics platform that enforces strict tenant isolation, data governance, and cost discipline while maintaining near real-time processing capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-region active-active design and disaster recovery strategy
- Per-tenant data isolation using storage prefixes and role-based access control
- Ingestion via Event Hubs; processing with Databricks and Delta Lake
- Data governance with Purview; encryption with customer-managed keys
- Cost optimization through auto-scaling and resource tagging

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Spark job configuration for per-tenant partitioning
spark.conf.set("spark.sql.shuffle.partitions", "200");
spark.conf.set("spark.databricks.delta.optimizeWrite.enabled", "true");

// Read tenant-specific data
df = spark.read.format("delta")
  .load(f"abfss://tenant-{tenant_id}@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/")
  .filter(col("tenant_id") == tenant_id);

// Write with tenant partitioning
df.write.partitionBy("tenant_id")
  .format("delta")
  .mode("append")
  .save(f"abfss://processed@storage.dfs.core.windows.net/analytics");
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical experience designing observable, scalable Azure telemetry pipelines with proper instrumentation, tenant isolation, and comprehensive monitoring integration for enterprise-grade media analytics platforms.

## Key Concepts

- OpenTelemetry integration across Event Hubs, Databricks, and Delta Lake
- End-to-end trace correlation and structured logging
- Adaptive sampling strategies and retention policies
- Tenant-level RBAC and data governance
- Comprehensive monitoring with Application Insights and Azure Monitor
- Real-time alerting and SLA management

